Write a React 19 custom hook in TypeScript (strict mode) for a bounded counter. Export it as

```ts
function useCounter(options?: {
  initial?: number;
  min?: number;
  max?: number;
  step?: number;
}): {
  count: number;
  increment: () => void;
  decrement: () => void;
  set: (n: number) => void;
  reset: () => void;
}
```

Defaults: `initial` 0, `min` -Infinity, `max` Infinity, `step` 1. You may assume `min <= max`.

Behaviour:
- `count` starts at `initial` clamped to `[min, max]`.
- `increment()` adds `step`, `decrement()` subtracts `step`, and `set(n)` sets the count to `n`; in every case the result is clamped to `[min, max]`.
- `reset()` sets the count back to `initial` clamped to `[min, max]`.
- Several calls in the same event handler all take effect: calling `increment()` twice in one handler adds `2 * step` (still clamped).
- `increment`, `decrement`, `set` and `reset` keep the same function identity across re-renders as long as the option values do not change, even when the caller passes a new options object literal on every render. (A `React.memo` child that receives one of them must not re-render just because `count` changed.)

Use only `react`. Answer with a single ```tsx code block containing only the module.
